      "narrative": "Lab 91 is a seed-stage semiconductor company founded by Anand Chamarthy that has raised $10M+ to date. The valuation analysis draws on a broader comp universe of 3 public semiconductor names (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private peers including Cerebras, SambaNova Systems, and Etched, though same-stage and narrow-filter comparables proved insufficient for direct peer median pricing.\n\nStage-progression methodology is the primary applicable lens here. Using the $10M raised as a valuation proxy and applying a typical 2.5x step-up multiplier from Seed to Series A yields an implied valuation of $25M. Public-comp anchoring cannot be applied without disclosed revenue; the semiconductor sector median P/S of 21.5x across the three public comps is calculable but not actionable for a pre-revenue or undisclosed-revenue entity. Peer-median triangulation was rejected due to insufficient same-stage or narrow-tier comparables within the semiconductor sector\u2014only 1 adjacent-stage peer emerged after filtering, well below the 3-datapoint minimum for statistical reliability.\n\nThe analysis supports a valuation range of $21M to $29M, centered on $25M. This range reflects modest downside and upside around the stage-progression result, but the conclusion rests on a single method. Key uncertainties include the absence of direct peer valuation data, no revenue baseline for public-comp work, and limited visibility into Lab 91's specific technical differentiation relative to the broader private-company peer set.",
