      "narrative": "Atomic Machines is a Series A-stage semiconductor company founded by Jeff Holden that has raised $144M to date. The valuation analysis draws on a comp universe of 3 public semiconductor peers (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private peers including SambaNova Systems, Cerebras, and Credo Semiconductor.\n\nGiven data limitations, only one valuation method proved applicable: stage-progression analysis. Using total raised ($144M) as a proxy for post-money valuation and applying a 2.0x step-up multiplier typical for Series A progression to the next stage, this method implies a valuation of $288M. A sensitivity range of \u00b115% yields a low of $245M and a high of $331M.\n\nThe peer-median approach could not be applied due to insufficient narrow-stage comparables in the Chips & Semiconductors sector; while 12 same-sector peers exist, fewer than three matched simultaneous filters for stage, adjacent valuation tier, and funding recency. Public-comp anchoring (median P/S of 21.5x across the three public comps) remains inapplicable absent the target's revenue disclosure. The analysis therefore rests on a single methodological pillar. Key uncertainties include the target's actual revenue run rate, recent funding terms, and comparative burn dynamics relative to the peer set. A more robust triangulation would require either disclosure of financial metrics or access to recent institutional term sheets from comparable Series A rounds.",
