      "narrative": "Amogy, a Series C climate and energy company, currently carries a $700M post-money valuation after raising over $300M. The valuation analysis triangulates against a peer universe of 9 comparable private frontier-tech firms in Climate & Energy, including KoBold Metals ($3.0B, Series C), Electric Hydrogen ($1.0B, Series C), and Redwood Materials ($6.0B, Series D), alongside one public comparable.\n\nTwo valuation methods were applied. Stage-progression analysis, using a 1.7x multiplier typical of Series C to next-stage advancement, implies a valuation of $1.2B. Peer-median analysis across the three closest comparables\u2014weighted by stage proximity\u2014yields a $3.0B median, with a range of $1.0B to $6.0B. The peer-median approach reflects broader market pricing for climate-tech at similar development stages, though heterogeneity in business models and capital intensity drives the wide spread.\n\nTriangulation across both methods produces a valuation range of $1.0B (low) to $3.4B (high), with a median of $2.1B. The $1.2B stage-progression result skews conservatively relative to peer medians, suggesting either that Amogy trades at a discount to comparable Series C peers or that stage-progression mechanics underweight sector momentum. Key uncertainties include product commercialization timeline, relative competitive positioning within the hydrogen-ammonia stack, and near-term capital requirements versus peer burn rates. The wide peer range reflects early-stage valuation dispersion typical of pre-revenue or early-revenue frontier-tech.",
