      "narrative": "Vention, a Series C-stage robotics and manufacturing automation company founded by Etienne Lacroix and Max Windisch, is evaluated against a universe of 9 comparable companies: 1 public (277810.KQ) and 8 private peers including Hadrian, Agile Robots, and Nimble Robotics.\n\nTwo valuation methods were applied. Stage-progression analysis uses the $95M in total capital raised as a baseline and applies a 1.7x step-up multiplier typical for Series C advancement, yielding $162M. Peer-median analysis draws from three same-stage, same-sector comparables with valuations ranging from $1.0B to $1.6B (Hadrian at $1.6B; Agile Robots and Nimble Robotics each at $1.0B), establishing a median of $1.0B. The wide dispersion between methods reflects heterogeneity in company maturity, capital intensity, and market positioning within the robotics sector.\n\nTriangulation across both methods supports a valuation range of $137M to $1.1B, with a median of $581M. The substantial gap between stage-progression and peer-median implies meaningful uncertainty around comparable company selection and relative positioning. Key variables affecting this range include customer concentration, unit economics, and path to profitability relative to the peer set. A tighter estimate would require greater clarity on Vention's revenue scale and growth trajectory relative to Hadrian and peer companies.",
