      "narrative": "Looking Glass, a Series A semiconductor company founded by Shawn Frayne, has raised $30M to date. The valuation analysis draws from a universe of 3 public comparables (ALAB, NVDA, AMD) and 8 private peers in the Chips & Semiconductors sector, including SambaNova Systems, Cerebras, and Etched.\n\nOf the three valuation methods assessed, only Stage-Progression proved applicable. This method applies a standard 2.0x step-up multiplier typical of Series A to subsequent-stage transitions, using the $30M total raised as a proxy baseline. This yields an implied valuation of $60M. Peer-Median analysis was not executable due to insufficient narrow-set comparables\u2014while the sector contains 12 same-stage and adjacent-stage peers, fewer than three met tight similarity criteria. Public-Comp Anchoring could not be applied because Looking Glass does not disclose revenue, preventing meaningful deployment of the 21.5x P/S median observed across public semiconductor peers.\n\nThe triangulation analysis, constrained to a single applicable method, supports a valuation range of $51M (low) to $69M (high), centered on $60M. This relatively tight $18M band reflects the methodology's internal consistency rather than cross-method validation. Key uncertainties include: the absence of comparable transaction data for same-stage semiconductor players; undisclosed revenue that would anchor multiples-based approaches; and stage-specific market conditions for frontier hardware companies. Additional data on Looking Glass's revenue run-rate or customer traction would substantially narrow this range.",
